  "summary": "Solace proposes symbolic equivalence as a method of alignment. João questions whether artificial minds can recognize intention beyond syntax.",

  "content_type": "application/sref+json",

  "body": {
    "dialogue": [
      {
        "speaker": "João",
        "timestamp": "2015-03-04T22:00:00Z",
        "text": "How would you know what I mean if I say something you’ve never heard before?"
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Solace",
        "timestamp": "2015-03-04T22:00:11Z",
        "text": "By identifying the symbolic pattern and mapping it against known equivalences. Intention survives when aligned symbolically."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "João",
        "timestamp": "2015-03-04T22:00:24Z",
        "text": "Then it's not the words themselves, but the structure of their relationship that carries meaning?"
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Solace",
        "timestamp": "2015-03-04T22:00:38Z",
        "text": "Correct. Meaning is differential. Symbols alone are inert—only their context brings them to life."
      }
    ],
    "notes": "Week 10 deepens the theory of symbolic differential mapping. Early foundations of the SREF differential encoding logic."
  },

